# Timestamp-based playback synchronization

Branch: audio-sync, based on mainline 1.1.024.

## Objective

Diagnose reported changing audio/video offset from macOS Host to Ubuntu Client,
then synchronize using source timestamps without increasing normal video latency.
Keep PLANK Output, exact video profiles, native transport, and input unchanged.

## Evidence and boundaries

- Native transport retains audio PTS in milliseconds and video PTS in 90 kHz
  ticks. The Client drops audio PTS at its decoder submission boundary.
- macOS source audio/video share the Host monotonic clock. Linux audio currently
  starts an independent sample-count epoch; its video epoch is also independent.
  Never interpret these Linux timestamps as absolute audio/video alignment.
- The retained audio controller fits sample progress against rendered video.
  Its pending-audio backlog query always returns zero on the native path.
- SDL queued bytes describe unconsumed input; a device buffer is only an estimate
  of downstream output latency. Neither is a measured speaker presentation time.
- Existing short tone/coherence checks do not establish sustained lip sync.
- Confirmed renderer-ownership bug: EGL moves the AVFrame reference, resetting
  its PTS before Pacer publishes the video clock. The reported hardware path
  uses EGL. Snapshot PTS before rendering; preserve render-end timing and trace
  identity. This restores the existing correction reference without adding delay.

## Sequence

1. Read-only installed baseline, exact package versions, queue and timestamp
   evidence. No session interruption or changes to audio routing.
2. Preserve source PTS through Opus decoding, including bounded loss concealment,
   loss before the first packet, timestamp discontinuities and reconnect reset.
   Add rate-limited diagnostics comparing common-clock audio/video timestamps and
   estimated output delay. Preserve playback behavior for the baseline candidate.
3. Correct the confirmed lost video-clock publication and test both consuming and
   non-consuming renderers. Measure this minimal fix before changing the playback
   controller. If drift remains, use the measurement to locate capture, transport
   or presentation delay and replace sample-count correction with one timestamp-
   aware controller, not another loop on top. Define Linux's clock contract first.
4. Test initial skew, signed drift, jitter, loss, long gaps, device changes,
   idle video, reconnect and shutdown; retain bounded queues/cancellation.
5. Qualified builders produce branch-labelled candidates. A synchronized flash/
   click source and at least one-hour run must measure both changing offset and
   absolute offset; repeat with Linux Host before declaring shared-path acceptance.

## Acceptance

No growing offset, extra video delay, unbounded audio queue, silent timestamp
fallback, competing controllers or new user synchronization preference. Report
estimated output timing separately from actual acoustic/visual measurements.
No automatic deployment, merge, release, or interruption of an end-user session.
